                            <a name="comment201"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: Six Months of Famine
</h4>

<p>
I hate this talk. It’s just such a huge fat duh. OF COURSE fewer people are getting newspapers delivered to their homes.<br />
<br />
Fewer people are going to the movies (but more people are watching movies). Fewer people are buying music (but digital downloads are doing just fine). MORE people are watching TV (but FEWER people are watching any one show).<br />
<br />
Re the Times’ steeper slippage relative to the Trib’s decline, and I’m only half-trying to be funny here, maybe what seems like a higher number of old folks in Pinellas County versus Hillsborough County has something to do with those numbers. They’re the ones who tend to take the paper because they come from a time when that’s just what interested aware educated people did. Not anymore.<br />
<br />
That doesn’t make younger people today dumb or any less informed.<br />
<br />
It just means it’s 2005.<br />
<br />
More options by definition translate into the fragmentation of any potential audience.<br />
<br />
Right?<br />
<br />
What’s interesting, though, is that the industry’s response to the current mega-shift in media – the last one was the onset of a-TV-in-every-home in the 50s – was NOT to fight fire with fire. On the contrary.<br />
<br />
This is David Halberstam in his foreword to the W.C. Heinz anthology called “What A Time It Was”: “Television had finally changed the structure of print – editors realized that print was no longer the quickest means of communication and that reporters had to go where television cameras could not go and answer the questions that television and its cameras raised but never answered. More, since all kinds of newspapers were dying because of television, the handful that remained understood, however involuntarily, that some measure of literary grace was now important.”<br />
<br />
More people, I think, are getting more information, and reading more stories, somehow, somewhere. MORE. Not less.<br />
<br />
Yet all the hand-wringing.<br />
<br />
All the blabber about the wind-sucking “mainstream media.” (Ugh.)<br />
<br />
All the short-sighted discussion of nuggets and blurbs and rails and other demeaning dumbed-down bullshit we seem to be shoveling out there in a frantic attempt to satisfy what is at this point clearly an antiquated form of measurement anyway.<br />
<br />
Where along the way in this shift did some boardroom knuckleheads look at these numbers and decide that people must now just want little tiny bits of information flicked at their faces?<br />
<br />
So let me throw this out there:<br />
<br />
OUR job hasn’t changed. At least it hasn’t changed nearly as much as the ad folks’ has. They’re the ones who have to figure out, and fast, how to make money off the different ways people now get their stories and their information.<br />
<br />
Our job’s still the same.<br />
<br />
Tell me a fucking story.<br />
<br />
Do it with some grace.
</p>
<div class="posted">Posted by:


		<a href="mailto:mkruse@sptimes.com">Kruse</a>
	

